  "meta": {
    "title": "Running remainder mod 3",
    "blurb": "Displays the value of the binary number read so far, modulo 3, after every bit. A Moore machine whose states are exactly the divisibility-by-3 DFA’s — the output is just the state’s name.",
    "inputs": [
      {
        "w": "1101",
        "out": "01001",
        "label": "13"
      },
      {
        "w": "11",
        "out": "010",
        "label": "3"
      },
      {
        "w": "111",
        "out": "0101",
        "label": "7"
      },
      {
        "w": "1000",
        "out": "01212",
        "label": "8"
      },
      {
        "w": "",
        "out": "0"
      }
    ],
    "library": {
      "author": {
        "login": "thethinkmachine"
      },
      "license": "CC-BY-4.0",
      "tags": [
        "binary",
        "remainder",
        "divisibility"
      ],
      "difficulty": "intro",
      "readme": "Every DFA is a Moore machine with a two-letter output alphabet — accept or not. Give the states richer outputs and the machine reports what it knows rather than a verdict: here, the whole remainder.\n\nReading **1101** (13) prints **01001**: the values of its prefixes are 0, 1, 3, 6 and 13, and their remainders mod 3 are 0, 1, 0, 0, 1. The last symbol is the answer the divisibility DFA would turn into accept or reject."
    }
  }
}
